  "contract": {
    "rootKinds": "A document must be an object or an array at the top level; a bare scalar is rejected.",
    "comments": "Every object key and every value carries at most one attached comment, queryable separately.",
    "numbers": "An unedited number keeps the spelling it was written with, while equality is mathematical.",
    "nesting": "Containers nested to acceptedDepth parse; one more container is rejected before deeper state is built.",
    "lineEndings": "A line comment ends at CR, LF or CRLF, and the terminator is not part of its body.",
    "lineOwnership": "For comment ownership a line ends at LF or CRLF only. A bare CR terminates a comment body but does not start a new line, so a comment reached after a bare CR belongs to the value before the separator, exactly as a same-line comment does. The user adopted this rule on 2026-09-25 after both implementations were measured to agree on it.",
    "roundTrip": "Emitting a parsed document must produce text that parses again, and every comment body listed for a case must be attached to some node afterwards, byte for byte.",
    "canonicalLayout": "Emitting a parsed document must produce exactly the recorded text, so indentation, separators, trailing commas and comment placement are one shared contract rather than two implementations that happen to agree today.",
    "rootShape": "Setting the document root is refused when the replacement is not a container, because a scalar root emits text the parser rejects.",
    "exactIdentityOnly": "A case whose expectation needs exact mathematical identity. The Rust crate satisfies every case; the TypeScript package stores IEEE 754 binary64 values, so cases carrying this flag are asserted there only through preserved source spelling, never through value equality."
